		tags = { "More articles" },
		aliases = { "Oxidation number" },
		summary = "In [[Chemistry|chemistry]], the **oxidation state**, or **oxidation number**, is the hypothetical charge of an atom assuming all of its [[Chemical bond|bonds]] to other atoms are fully ionic. It describes the degree of [[Redox|oxidation]] (loss of [[Electron|electrons]]) of an [[Atom|atom]] in a [[Chemical compound|chemical compound]]. Conceptually, the oxidation state may be positive, negative or zero. Oxidation state is useful for estimating the distribution of [[Electric charge|electric charge]] within molecules, especially for those containing primarily [[Ionic bonding|ionic bonds]], and less so for those with primarily [[Covalent bond|covalent bonds]].",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The oxidation state of an atom is capable of qualitative explanation of patterns in chemical reactions. It does not represent an atom's actual charge. The oxidation states of atoms in a given compound may vary depending on the [[Electronegativity|electronegativity]] scale used. It is also relevant to the nomenclature conventions of [[Inorganic compound|inorganic compounds]]. Oxidation states are typically represented by [[Integer|integers]] which may be positive, zero, or negative. In some cases, the average oxidation state of an element is a fraction, such as 8/3 for [[Iron|iron]] in magnetite Fe3O4 (see below). The highest known oxidation state is +9, displayed by [[Iridium|iridium]] in the tetroxoiridium(IX) cation (IrO4+). An even higher +10 is predicted to be possible for [[Platinum|platinum]] in tetroxoplatinum(X), PtO4(2+). The lowest oxidation state is −5, as for [[Boron|boron]] in Al3BC and [[Gallium|gallium]] in pentamagnesium digallide (Mg5Ga2).",
